    "body": "Kuehne+Nagel and Amazon have tested a shared charging model that lets Kuehne+Nagel’s electric trucks charge up at Amazon facilities in the UK.\nWhat’s Related\nIn the pilot, Kuehne+Nagel, which works as a logistics partner to Amazon, got access to existing fast chargers at Amazon sites its trucks already visit. The trucks plugged in while they were being loaded and unloaded, so drivers didn’t have to go out of their way to find a charger.\nKuehne+Nagel said the pilot reached a 90% charging success rate. That number is the share of planned charging stops that ended in a successful session. Trucks also gained 40% in battery charge during their time on site, the company said.\n“Our pilot addresses two key challenges: it expands the charging network, which makes it easier to plan electric truck journeys; and it ensures that charging infrastructure is sufficiently utilized to deliver a return on investment,” said John de Dryver, global strategy and sustainability manager at Kuehne+Nagel.\nHow it works\nCharging stations for heavy trucks take a big upfront investment, so they need steady use to make financial sense. Sharing chargers that are already on site at Amazon helps keep them busy. It also gives outside carriers access to fast truck charging, which Kuehne+Nagel called a key barrier to running electric line-haul routes.\nKuehne+Nagel said Amazon’s UK sites made sense for two reasons. First, the chargers are fast. They can typically take a truck battery from 20% to 80% in about 45 minutes, which fits inside a normal loading or unloading window. Second, Kuehne+Nagel trucks already stop at Amazon fulfillment centers regularly, so there were no extra miles involved.\nAccess to charging still a blocker\nKuehne+Nagel said the range and charging speeds of heavy-duty electric trucks still don’t fully match what day-to-day operations require. It added that reliable access to charging is still a key bottleneck.\n“One of the most encouraging findings from the pilot was that the vast majority of unsuccessful charging attempts were not linked to the charging technology or the operational model itself,” said Matthew Scoble, road logistics cross-border specialist at Kuehne+Nagel. “This reinforces our belief that the model we piloted is a viable and scalable solution, with the potential to achieve near 100% charging success rates under normal operating conditions.”",
